# Appendix C — Editor integration details

This appendix maps Literate's host-independent model onto VS Code's extension and
Markdown APIs. The architectural account is in
[The editor-side project repository](editor_project_repository.html),
[Fragments as editor symbols](fragments_as_editor_symbols.html),
[Previewing the explanation](previewing_the_explanation.html), and
[Extension activation and public surface](extension_activation_surface.html).
Here the emphasis is registration, adapter ownership, and the contracts a contributor
needs when tracing an editor request.

## Declarative and runtime surfaces

The package manifest makes features discoverable before extension code runs. It
declares the Fragment Explorer view and refresh menu, public commands, configuration
schemas, the pending-completion keybinding, Markdown-it participation, and the local
browser scripts used by Markdown preview.

Activation then creates the session-owned objects behind that surface. The exported
`activate` function is the extension entry point. It returns an `extendMarkdownIt`
hook, which is the runtime entry point used by the Markdown host.

The manifest contains no imperative activation list because the host can infer
activation from contributed commands, views, and Markdown support. Command IDs,
view IDs, and setting keys should be treated as public compatibility boundaries.

## Activation order

Activation follows dependency order rather than chapter or generated-file order:

1. construct the shared `FragmentRepository` and attach it to the extension context;
2. register publication commands around the repository's serialized publication
   boundary;
3. register mapped language features around a source-capture function, not around
   unrestricted repository internals;
4. perform an awaited, read-only initial project scan;
5. create the preview-navigation capability handler;
6. register fragment authoring commands, the Fragment Explorer, and fragment symbol
   providers;
7. return the Markdown-it extension hook.

A failed initial read is contained. Providers and commands still register and can
report or recover from the current project state. Initial activation never generates
source or HTML. See [One generator, several hosts](one_generator_several_hosts.html)
for the host boundary and [Changing files safely](changing_files_safely.html) for the
separate write transaction.

## The project repository adapter

`FragmentRepository` maintains one coherent editor-side view per workspace folder.
Its published state includes accepted source snapshots, fragment records, Markdown
token locations needed by existing commands, preview theme state, generated-output
ownership, and the diagnostics owned by Literate.

Source capture follows these rules:

- discover all eligible `.literate` paths, including unlinked chapters;
- apply the same excluded-directory and semantic ordering policies as the CLI;
- prefer an exact open text buffer over disk, including an empty buffer;
- include new file-backed buffers that have a stable workspace-relative identity;
- exclude untitled documents from project membership;
- discover declared templates through the engine, then capture them with the same
  open-buffer precedence;
- treat genuine absence as a missing input, while propagating other acquisition
  failures rather than constructing a partial snapshot.

Repository work is serialized. Providers request a read-only refresh. Explicit
processing, accepted source changes, relevant filesystem events, and added workspace
folders may request generation. Publication joins the queue for capture and lifetime
coordination but uses its own helper pipeline. Preview rendering is synchronous and
uses a separate in-memory overlay on the last discovered membership.

The repository translates engine diagnostics into one workspace-scoped
`DiagnosticCollection`. Replacement is whole-snapshot replacement: repairing a
project clears obsolete entries, and refreshing one workspace does not clear another.

## The Markdown parser state bridge

The parser-state bridge connects Markdown parsing to editor adapters. `grabberPlugin`
installs a Markdown-it core rule named `grabit`
before Markdown-it's `normalize` rule. When the parser receives an environment
object, that rule stores the current parser `StateCore` as `env.gstate`, then returns
without consuming or rewriting the token stream.

The captured state gives the extension the actual token array, token maps, fence
contents, and parser environment associated with one document. A `GrabbedState`
joins that parser state to the document's workspace-relative name and full editor URI.
The repository parses documents in engine-determined order with this plug-in and
retains one such record per source.

The bridge is intentionally not a second fragment parser. The engine's inspection
result decides which declarations were accepted. Later adapter code matches each
accepted definition location to the corresponding captured fence token by document
and opening line. This join supplies concrete Markdown token locations to legacy
commands and views without allowing a token scan to overrule semantic validation.

The same plug-in is installed in the Markdown parser returned by `extendMarkdownIt`.
That makes host preview token state available to Literate's fence and document
wrappers while preserving the built-in Markdown pipeline. The contract is narrow:

- capture the parser-owned `StateCore` in `env.gstate`;
- do not mutate source, normalize it early, render, or generate files;
- let the next Markdown-it core rule continue;
- use captured tokens only in combination with current engine-approved identities.

This bridge is therefore an adapter to parser state, not the source of fragment
semantics. Its complete implementation appears later in this appendix.

## Fragment provider registration

Fragment-oriented providers register for the Markdown language because `.literate`
files use VS Code's built-in Markdown support. Registration is broader than action:
each adapter locates the current workspace, refreshes read-only state, and declines
when the cursor is outside a meaningful fragment occurrence.

The registered fragment providers are:

- a `CompletionItemProvider`, triggered by `<`, which offers family names only after
  an unmatched `<<` before the cursor;
- a `DefinitionProvider`, which selects the base family definition;
- a `ReferenceProvider`, which returns semantic references and prose mentions, with
  declarations controlled by the caller's request;
- a `HoverProvider`, which presents bounded unexpanded family content;
- a `RenameProvider`, which previews and reparses a complete project-wide name edit;
- a `CodeActionProvider`, which offers a current-snapshot fix for a verified missing
  reference.

The providers use raw UTF-16 editor coordinates. Rename edits only the inner name
range, leaving chevrons and operators intact. A read-only refresh may inspect unsaved
buffers but does not write generated output.

Mapped programming-language providers use a narrower document selector: local
file-backed Markdown documents matching `**/*.literate`. Runtime checks additionally
require an open document, a trusted local workspace, an accepted named-fragment body,
a configured canonical language, and a position that maps into an in-memory output.
Their registrations are listed in
[Coordinating language features in the editor](coordinating_language_features.html).

## Command registration and effect levels

Activation registers command implementations and places every returned disposable in
the extension context.

- `literate.process` asks the repository to capture and process workspace projects
  with write intent. Only a complete valid plan reaches output preflight and commit.
- `literate.create_fragment_for_tag` edits the active document to define the selected
  family.
- `literate.split_fragment` closes the current fence and opens an append below the
  selected position, preserving the current language and family.
- `fragmentExplorer.refreshEntry` fires tree invalidation so VS Code requests visible
  children again; it does not generate files.
- `literate.restartLanguageServers` resets mapped-language sessions so later requests
  may create new processes.
- publication commands begin explicit, trusted export flows and do not run during
  activation or ordinary preview.

The manifest also declares `literate.cancelAutomaticCompletion`. Its keybinding is
enabled only while an automatic request is pending and ordinary editor widgets do not
already own Escape.

## Fragment Explorer adapters

The Explorer contribution uses the public view ID `fragmentExplorer`.
`FragmentExplorer` owns the view registration, view instance, refresh command, and
change subscription. `FragmentNodeProvider` implements `TreeDataProvider<FragmentNode>`
and emits `onDidChangeTreeData` when its cached presentation should be queried again.

Top-level children represent workspace folders and accepted families. Expanding a
family asks for its direct unexpanded references; selecting a resolved item opens its
first accepted contribution. Missing targets remain visible without an invented
location. Child discovery is lazy, so creating the view does not recursively unfold
the graph.

The tree mirrors graph edges and currently has no active-path cycle marker or global
expansion budget. The bounded inline browser explorer is a different adapter with a
different safety contract. See [Exploring the fragment graph](exploring_fragment_graph.html)
and [Appendix D — Browser programs](appendix_browser_programs.html).

## Markdown preview integration

`extendMarkdownIt` installs three cooperating pieces on the host parser:

1. `grabberPlugin` captures parser state in the environment;
2. `installPreviewFences` wraps fence rendering;
3. `installPreviewDocument` wraps complete document rendering with project theme,
   semantic links, annotations, and optional exploration data.

Fence handling narrows in this order:

- fence headers containing `SETTINGS` currently render nothing in preview, while
  only an exactly trimmed `SETTINGS` info string is interpreted as project settings;
- a plain `mermaid` fence becomes an escaped source container for the local browser
  renderer;
- an engine-approved named contribution receives stable identity, navigation, and
  semantic reference decoration only after the current token matches path, line,
  header, role, language, and content;
- every other fence delegates to the renderer that was installed before Literate.

A named fragment whose language is Mermaid remains source code; only a plain Mermaid
fence is a diagram. A stale or malformed declaration remains safely rendered code
without semantic links.

Preview builds a read-only in-memory project by overlaying open buffers and the exact
source currently given to the parser on the repository's last discovered membership.
It does not wait for filesystem discovery and never acquires generation authority.
See [Previewing the explanation](previewing_the_explanation.html).

## Preview navigation adapter

Cross-document preview links use a temporary opaque capability rather than a command
URI containing a path and line number. The activation-owned URI handler mints a
capability only for a trusted current workspace and an accepted logical publication
target.

When activated, the handler rechecks its scheme and authority, token shape, session
membership, expiry, workspace identity, trust, current project semantics, and
canonical path. It then resolves the logical target against fresh source before
opening and selecting it. The rendered document cannot supply an arbitrary command,
filename, or stale line as editor authority.

Generated HTML uses ordinary relative chapter links for the same logical identities.
The screen renderer therefore owns target meaning, while the editor adapter owns the
privilege of opening a source document.

## Routine VS Code conversions

Host adapters should remain mechanical and fail closed:

- engine zero-based line and UTF-16 column values become `Position` and `Range`;
- workspace-relative source paths become URIs by joining them to the owning folder,
  never by accepting an arbitrary URI from rendered or server data;
- pure occurrence records become `Location`, `CompletionItem`, `Hover`,
  `WorkspaceEdit`, `CodeAction`, `TreeItem`, or `Diagnostic` values only after their
  feature-specific checks pass;
- cancellation tokens are checked before and after asynchronous refreshes;
- editor document versions are retained whenever a later result could propose an edit;
- missing or stale evidence returns no provider result rather than a guessed range.

These conversions should not redefine fragment grammar, ordering, graph validity,
projection, or output policy. Those belong to the engine and pure helper modules.

## Trust and configuration boundaries

Read-only fragment inspection can remain available without starting an executable.
Capabilities with greater authority are checked where exercised:

- preview-to-editor navigation requires a trusted current workspace and a valid
  temporary capability;
- mapped language servers require trusted local file workspaces and restricted global
  executable configuration;
- publication requires a trusted local desktop workspace and independently validated
  process and path settings;
- generated-file replacement requires a complete plan plus destination preflight and
  transaction commit.

Workspace Trust is therefore not a substitute for path, protocol, freshness, or
transaction validation. Each adapter must enforce the narrower boundary attached to
its own effect.

## Disposal and ownership

Every provider, command, listener, view, diagnostic collection, timer, URI handler,
server coordinator, and repository enters an explicit owner. Extension-level
resources are attached to `context.subscriptions`.

Disposal closes input before clearing state: new repository work is refused, timers
are cancelled, preview capabilities are invalidated, providers and listeners are
released, diagnostics and bounded caches are cleared, and workspace snapshots are
dropped. Language sessions have a bounded shutdown sequence. Publication helpers
receive graceful cancellation, but the editor continues waiting if transaction
cleanup outlives its deadline rather than risking an abandoned rollback. Cleanup is
idempotent because normal deactivation and failure paths may meet the same resource.

## Tracing an editor request

For a fragment-name operation, follow this route:

1. provider registration in the activation assembly;
2. read-only repository refresh;
3. engine inspection and pure symbol collection;
4. exact occurrence lookup;
5. feature-specific conversion to an editor result.

For a Markdown preview problem, follow:

1. the returned `extendMarkdownIt` hook;
2. the parser-state bridge and `env.gstate`;
3. the fence and document wrappers;
4. the repository's synchronous preview overlay;
5. screen rendering and the relevant browser program.

For mapped programming-language behavior, begin with
[Coordinating language features in the editor](coordinating_language_features.html),
then use [Appendix E — Language-server protocol details](appendix_language_server_protocol.html)
for transport and reply contracts.

## Provider implementation reference

The following fragments are the mechanical VS Code adapters behind the symbol
operations described in Chapter 14. They share the repository and symbol semantics
already explained above; the code here records exact API conversion and registration.

## Fragment-name completion provider

Fragment names form a project vocabulary, but an author should not have to recall
every name exactly. The completion adapter participates in Markdown editing and
responds only after an unmatched opening `<<`, leaving ordinary prose and projected
programming-language completion to their own providers.

``` ts : <<register fragment name completion>>=
const completionItemProvider =
  vscode.languages.registerCompletionItemProvider('markdown', {
    <<provide fragment name completions>>
}, '<');
context.subscriptions.push(completionItemProvider);
```

### Providing completion items

Inside an unfinished fragment tag, the provider offers a `CompletionItem` for
each known fragment in the project. Elsewhere it returns no fragment suggestions,
leaving ordinary code completion to the configured language server.

``` ts : <<provide fragment name completions>>=
async provideCompletionItems(
  document : vscode.TextDocument,
  position: vscode.Position
)
{
  const prefix = document.lineAt(position.line).text.slice(0, position.character);
  if (!/\x3c\x3c[^>]*$/.test(prefix)) { return []; }
```

`begin collecting fragment completions` creates the result, while
`require an owning workspace for completion` declines documents that do not belong
to a workspace project.

``` ts : <<provide fragment name completions>>=+
  <<begin collecting fragment completions>>
  <<require an owning workspace for completion>>
```

With project authority established, `refresh fragments before completion` reads a
current, read-only repository snapshot.

``` ts : <<provide fragment name completions>>=+
  <<refresh fragments before completion>>
```

`offer each fragment as a completion` turns the known family map into editor items.

``` ts : <<provide fragment name completions>>=+
  <<offer each fragment as a completion>>
  return completionItems;
}
```

#### Setting up variables

The result is an array of `CompletionItem` values.

``` ts : <<begin collecting fragment completions>>=
let completionItems : Array<vscode.CompletionItem> =
    new Array<vscode.CompletionItem>();
```

#### Workspace folder for TextDocument

The shared workspace helper identifies the folder that owns the document. Without
one, no project fragment vocabulary is available and the provider returns no items.

``` ts : <<require an owning workspace for completion>>=
const workspaceFolder : vscode.WorkspaceFolder | undefined = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
if(!workspaceFolder) { return []; }
```

#### Retrieving fragments of project

Completion refreshes the fragment repository from current editor buffers before
reading its map. This uses the same serialized processing queue as generation,
but the default is read-only: asking for suggestions never writes source or HTML.
Literate content changes independently request generation, including changes
beginning with an opening chevron.

``` ts : <<refresh fragments before completion>>=
await theOneRepository.processLiterateFiles(workspaceFolder);
let fragments = theOneRepository.getFragments(workspaceFolder).map;
```

#### Creating the CompletionItems

Each known family becomes a reference-kind completion item. Its name is the inserted
text, while bounded unexpanded source appears as detail so similarly named families
can be distinguished before selection.

``` ts : <<offer each fragment as a completion>>=
for(const fragmentName of fragments.keys())
{
  const fragment : FragmentInformation | undefined = fragments.get(fragmentName);
  if(!fragment) {
    continue;
  }
  const fragmentCompletion = new vscode.CompletionItem(fragmentName);
  fragmentCompletion.detail = fragment.code;
  fragmentCompletion.kind = vscode.CompletionItemKind.Reference;
  completionItems.push(fragmentCompletion);
}
```

## Fragment hover provider

Hover lets an author inspect a referenced family's unexpanded source without leaving
the current explanation. `FragmentHoverProvider` adapts that read-only lookup to
VS Code's `HoverProvider` contract.

``` ts : <<fragment hover provider>>=
export class FragmentHoverProvider implements vscode.HoverProvider {
  readonly fragmentRepository : FragmentRepository;
  constructor(repository : FragmentRepository)
  {
    this.fragmentRepository = repository;
  }
  <<provide fragment hover>>
}
```

The `FragmentHoverProvider` implements `provideHover`. This will create the
`Hover` item if under the current cursor position there is a fragment, including
its opening and closing double chevrons. Refresh is read-only and includes
unsaved buffers; cancelled requests return no hover.

``` ts : <<provide fragment hover>>=
public async provideHover(
  document : vscode.TextDocument,
  position : vscode.Position,
  token: vscode.CancellationToken
)
{
  if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
  <<require an owning workspace for hover>>
  await this.fragmentRepository.processLiterateFiles(document);
  if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
  <<read the hovered source line>>
  <<show unexpanded fragment source>>
  return null;
}
```

Fragment tags are lexical single-line forms, so hover begins with the current source
line and the cursor's exact UTF-16 position.

``` ts : <<read the hovered source line>>=
const currentLine = document.lineAt(position.line);
```

The document must belong to a workspace project before its fragment family can be
resolved. Otherwise the provider returns no hover.

``` ts : <<require an owning workspace for hover>>=
const workspaceFolder : vscode.WorkspaceFolder | undefined = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
if(!workspaceFolder) { return null; }
```

An exact non-root occurrence yields a `Hover` containing the family's unexpanded
source as a fenced `MarkdownString`. Definitions, appends, missing occurrences, and
cancelled requests yield no hover.

``` ts : <<show unexpanded fragment source>>=
let fragmentLocation = this.fragmentRepository.getFragmentTagLocation(document, currentLine, position);
if(fragmentLocation.valid && fragmentLocation.fragment && !fragmentLocation.root)
{
  let fragment = fragmentLocation.fragment;
  let range = fragmentLocation.range;
  return new vscode.Hover(
    new vscode.MarkdownString(`~~~ ${fragment.lang}\n${fragment.code}\n~~~`, true),
    range
    );
}
```


## Definition provider

The definition provider maps an exact occurrence to the family's base definition.
It returns a `vscode.Location` only after a read-only refresh confirms both the
occurrence and destination.

``` ts : <<fragment definition provider>>=
export class LiterateDefinitionProvider implements vscode.DefinitionProvider
{
    <<construct the definition provider>>
    <<provide a fragment definition>>
}
```

`LiterateDefinitionProvider` receives the shared repository as a parameter property.

```  ts  : <<construct the definition provider>>=
constructor(
    private readonly repository : FragmentRepository
) {}
```

The provider refreshes a read-only snapshot before lookup and honors cancellation.
Definition locations use the same exact tag ranges as references and hover.

```  ts  : <<provide a fragment definition>>=
public async provideDefinition
    (
        document : vscode.TextDocument,
        position : vscode.Position,
        token : vscode.CancellationToken
    )
{
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const workspaceFolder = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
    if(!workspaceFolder) { return null; }
    await this.repository.processLiterateFiles(document);
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const fragmentLocation = this.repository.getFragmentTagLocation(
        document,
        document.lineAt(position),
        position
    );
    if(!fragmentLocation.valid) { return null; }
    const definition = this.repository.getFragmentOccurrences(workspaceFolder).find(
        occurrence => occurrence.name === fragmentLocation.name && occurrence.kind === 'definition'
    );
    if(!definition || token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    return new vscode.Location(
        vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, definition.path),
        new vscode.Range(definition.line, definition.column, definition.line, definition.endColumn)
    );
}
```

### Registering the definition provider

``` ts : <<register the definition provider>>=
context.subscriptions.push(
    vscode.languages.registerDefinitionProvider(
        'markdown',
        new LiterateDefinitionProvider(theOneRepository)
    )
);
```

## Reference provider

The reference provider returns semantic uses of the selected family, optionally
including its declarations according to the editor request.

``` ts : <<fragment reference provider>>=
export class LiterateReferenceProvider implements vscode.ReferenceProvider
{
    <<construct the reference provider>>
    <<provide fragment references>>
}
```

`LiterateReferenceProvider` receives the same shared repository as a parameter
property.

```  ts  : <<construct the reference provider>>=
constructor(
    private readonly repository : FragmentRepository
) {}
```

The request supplies a document, position, declaration policy, and cancellation
token. Cancellation is checked before and after refreshing the read-only symbol
snapshot and again before locations are returned.

```  ts  : <<provide fragment references>>=
public async provideReferences(
    document : vscode.TextDocument,
    position : vscode.Position,
    context: { includeDeclaration : boolean},
    token : vscode.CancellationToken
)
{
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const workspaceFolder = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
    if(!workspaceFolder) { return null; }
    await this.repository.processLiterateFiles(document);
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const fragmentLocation = this.repository.getFragmentTagLocation(
        document,
        document.lineAt(position),
        position
    );

    if(!fragmentLocation.valid) { return null; }
    const locations = await this.repository.getReferenceLocations(
        workspaceFolder,
        fragmentLocation.name,
        context.includeDeclaration
    );
    return token.isCancellationRequested ? null : locations;

}
```

### Registering the reference provider

``` ts : <<register the reference provider>>=
context.subscriptions.push(
    vscode.languages.registerReferenceProvider(
        'markdown',
        new LiterateReferenceProvider(theOneRepository)
    )
);
```

## Rename provider

Rename uses the shared symbol planner, so only explicit fragment names change.
Validation rejects ambiguous definitions, collisions and changes to root status
without touching prose, filenames or generated output.

The provider returns a proposed workspace edit; it does not apply edits or write
generated files itself. VS Code owns applying the edit. A resulting literate
content-change event can then trigger the ordinary generation path.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant User as Author
    participant VS as VS Code
    participant Provider as Rename provider
    participant Repo as Fragment repository
    participant Planner as Symbol planner
    User->>VS: Rename a fragment
    VS->>Provider: Prepare rename at cursor
    Provider->>Repo: Refresh from current buffers
    Repo-->>Provider: Exact occurrence and source snapshot
    Provider->>Planner: Validate same-name plan
    Planner-->>Provider: Unique valid definition
    Provider-->>VS: Inner-name range and placeholder
    User->>VS: Enter new name
    VS->>Provider: Request rename edits
    Provider->>Repo: Refresh again
    Provider->>Planner: Plan rename from fresh sources
    Note over Planner: Validate names, collisions and Markdown structure
    alt Valid plan
        Planner-->>Provider: Name-only edit ranges
        Provider-->>VS: WorkspaceEdit
        VS->>VS: Apply edits to source documents
        VS->>Repo: Literate content-change events
    else Unsafe rename
        Planner-->>Provider: Validation error
        Provider-->>VS: Reject rename without partial edits
    end
```

Cancellation and positions that do not identify a defined fragment stop before
returning edits. Those early exits are omitted here to keep the successful
interaction and validation failure visible.

``` ts : <<rename provider class>>=
export class LiterateRenameProvider implements vscode.RenameProvider
{
    constructor(public readonly repository : FragmentRepository) {}

    <<prepare fragment rename>>
    <<fragment rename provider>>
}
```

Preparing a rename refreshes the read-only repository from open buffers. The
shared lookup supplies the inner name range, excluding the tag delimiters.
A no-op plan also verifies that the selected symbol has one base definition.

``` ts : <<prepare fragment rename>>=
public async prepareRename(
    document : vscode.TextDocument,
    position : vscode.Position,
    token : vscode.CancellationToken
)
{
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const workspaceFolder = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
    if(!workspaceFolder) { return null; }
    await this.repository.processLiterateFiles(document);
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const location = this.repository.getFragmentTagLocation(
        document, document.lineAt(position.line), position
    );
    if(!location.valid || !location.fragment) { return null; }
    planFragmentRename(
        this.repository.getSourceDocuments(workspaceFolder), location.name, location.name
    );
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    return { range: location.rangeExclusive, placeholder: location.name };
}
```

The actual request refreshes again: the user may have edited a buffer since
preparation. Cancellation discards the whole result, never a partial edit set.
Planner errors propagate to the editor as actionable rename failures.

``` ts : <<fragment rename provider>>=
public async provideRenameEdits(
    document : vscode.TextDocument,
    position : vscode.Position,
    newName : string,
    token : vscode.CancellationToken
)
{
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const workspaceFolder = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
    if(!workspaceFolder) { return null; }
    await this.repository.processLiterateFiles(document);
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    const location = this.repository.getFragmentTagLocation(
        document, document.lineAt(position.line), position
    );
    if(!location.valid || !location.fragment) { return null; }
    const plan = planFragmentRename(
        this.repository.getSourceDocuments(workspaceFolder), location.name, newName
    );
    <<adapt fragment rename plan>>
}
```

Planner paths are canonical workspace-relative source paths. Convert their
UTF-16 name ranges to editor edits without reading disk or applying changes.

``` ts : <<adapt fragment rename plan>>=
const workspaceEdit = new vscode.WorkspaceEdit();
for(const edit of plan)
{
    if(token.isCancellationRequested) { return null; }
    workspaceEdit.replace(
        vscode.Uri.joinPath(workspaceFolder.uri, edit.path),
        new vscode.Range(edit.line, edit.startColumn, edit.line, edit.endColumn),
        edit.newText
    );
}
return token.isCancellationRequested ? null : workspaceEdit;
```

## Missing-reference code action provider

Missing-fragment diagnostics carry a stable code rather than relying on their
human-readable message. Quick fixes refresh the read-only repository, then
require an exact unresolved reference in the current source snapshot. Cancelled
requests and buffers changed during refresh return no actions.

The edit belongs to the requested document URI, not the active editor. It adds
an empty definition after the containing, closed top-level fragment fence.
Unsafe insertion sites (including nested or unclosed fences) are declined rather
than invoking the active-editor create command. VS Code applies the proposed
edit; this provider neither applies edits nor generates output. Tilde fences permit
backticks in fragment names and language identifiers without breaking the header.

``` ts : <<code action provider class>>=
export class LiterateCodeActionProvider implements vscode.CodeActionProvider
{
    constructor(public readonly repository : FragmentRepository) {}

    public async provideCodeActions(
        document : vscode.TextDocument,
        _ : vscode.Range,
        context : vscode.CodeActionContext,
        token : vscode.CancellationToken
    ) : Promise<vscode.CodeAction[]>
    {
        <<code action snapshot validation>>
        const occurrences = this.repository.getFragmentOccurrences(folder);
        const actions: vscode.CodeAction[] = [];
        const offered = new Set<string>();
        for(const diagnostic of diagnostics)
        {
            <<code action unresolved reference and insertion site>>
            <<code action proposed edit>>
        }
        return token.isCancellationRequested ? [] : actions;
    }
}
```

### Match a stable source snapshot

Filter diagnostics before refreshing the repository, then verify both the requested
buffer and any open copy against the captured text and version. The repository's
source must agree too: an action derived from stale occurrences could edit the
wrong range. These checks share the method's scope without adding an async boundary.

``` ts : <<code action snapshot validation>>=
const diagnostics = context.diagnostics.filter(diag =>
    (typeof diag.code === 'object' ? diag.code.value : diag.code) === 'missing-fragment');
if(token.isCancellationRequested || !diagnostics.length ||
    (context.only && !context.only.contains(vscode.CodeActionKind.QuickFix))) { return []; }
const folder = determineWorkspaceFolder(document);
if(!folder) { return []; }
const version = document.version;
const text = document.getText();
await this.repository.processLiterateFiles(document);
if(token.isCancellationRequested || document.version !== version || document.getText() !== text) { return []; }
const documentPath = path.posix.relative(folder.uri.path, document.uri.path);
const sources = this.repository.getSourceDocuments(folder);
if(sources.find(source => source.path === documentPath)?.text !== text ||
    vscode.workspace.textDocuments.some(open => open.uri.toString() === document.uri.toString() &&
        (open.version !== version || open.getText() !== text))) { return []; }
```

### Require an exact reference and a safe insertion site

One fix per unresolved name is enough. Wildcard roots and already-defined names
are not creation candidates. Matching source text guards the diagnostic range;
checking a closed, top-level fence ensures insertion cannot capture existing prose
or code. Re-parsing the proposed header protects fragment identity.

``` ts : <<code action unresolved reference and insertion site>>=
const reference = occurrences.find(item => item.path === documentPath && item.kind === 'reference' &&
    item.line === diagnostic.range.start.line && item.column === diagnostic.range.start.character);
if(!reference || offered.has(reference.name) || reference.name.endsWith('.*') ||
    occurrences.some(item => item.name === reference.name && item.kind === 'definition')) { continue; }
const range = new vscode.Range(reference.line, reference.column, reference.line, reference.endColumn);
if(document.getText(range) !== `${OPENING}${reference.name}${CLOSING}`) { continue; }
const usage = this.repository.getTokenAtPosition(document, range).token;
if(!usage || usage.type !== 'fence' || !usage.map || usage.level !== 0) { continue; }
const header = parseFragmentHeader(usage.info);
const closing = document.lineAt(usage.map[1] - 1).text.trim();
if(!header || !/^(`{3,}|~{3,})$/.test(closing) || closing[0] !== usage.markup[0] ||
    closing.length < usage.markup.length) { continue; }
const info = `${header.language} : ${OPENING}${reference.name}${CLOSING}=`;
if(parseFragmentHeader(info)?.name !== reference.name) { continue; }
```

### Offer the edit without applying it

Preserve the document's line ending and attach the edit to its URI, even if another
editor is active. Only a fully constructed action marks a name as offered; VS Code
retains responsibility for applying the edit and presenting its diagnostic.

``` ts : <<code action proposed edit>>=
const eol = document.eol === vscode.EndOfLine.CRLF ? '\r\n' : '\n';
const position = usage.map[1] < document.lineCount ? new vscode.Position(usage.map[1], 0)
    : document.positionAt(text.length);
const edit = new vscode.WorkspaceEdit();
edit.insert(document.uri, position, `${eol}~~~ ${info}${eol}~~~${eol}`);
const action = new vscode.CodeAction(`Create fragment for ${OPENING}${reference.name}${CLOSING}`,
    vscode.CodeActionKind.QuickFix);
action.diagnostics = [diagnostic];
action.edit = edit;
actions.push(action);
offered.add(reference.name);
```

## Markdown parser-state bridge implementation

The `grabit` core rule captures the current Markdown-it `StateCore` in the parser
environment before normalization. Returning `false` leaves the token stream
untouched and allows the remaining core rules to continue; later adapters combine
the captured tokens with engine-approved fragment identities.

```ts : <<assemble the Markdown parser state bridge.*>>= ./src/grabber.ts $
import MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
import Core = require('markdown-it/lib/parser_core');

export function grabberPlugin(md : MarkdownIt) {
    const grabit : Core.RuleCore = state => {
        if (state.env) {
            state.env.gstate = state;
        }
        return false;
    };

    md.core.ruler.before('normalize', 'grabit', grabit);
};
```
